AB Advanced Micro Devices, Inc. (AMD) has recently launched the MI300A accelerated processing unit (APU), which integrates central processing unit (CPU) and graphical processing unit (GPU) compute in a single chip with unified high bandwidth memory (HBM). This study assesses the AMD Instinct(TM) MI300A capabilities and examines how this new architecture handles real-world generative artificial intelligence (AI) workloads. While performance data for large language model (LLM) use cases exists for the MI250X and MI300X, to the best of our knowledge, such assessments are absent for the MI300A. We apply a knowledge distillation (KD) use case to distil the knowledge of the Mistral-Small-24B-Base-2501 teacher model to a student model that is 50% sparse using a 2:4 sparsity pattern. The results show quasi-linear scaling of raw performances on up to 256 APUs. Lastly, we discuss the challenges, research, and practical implications.
