TI AES Cryptography Enabled Responsible Federated Foundation Model Using
   Transformer LLM and LSTM for Smart Grid IIoT Networks
SO IEEE INTERNET OF THINGS JOURNAL
LA English
DT Article
DE Cryptography; Federated learning; Load forecasting; Mathematical models;
   Long short term memory; Forecasting; Smart meters; Transformers; Load
   modeling; Vectors; Cybersecurity; distributed deep learning; foundation
   model; Industrial IoT (IIoT); large language model (LLM); long
   short-term memory (LSTM); responsible artificial intelligence
   (Responsible AI); smart grid
AB The use of supervisory control and data acquisition (SCADA) and advanced metering infrastructure (AMI) systems in smart grid-based Industrial Internet of Things (SG-IIoT) networks for proper energy supply is noteworthy. Inaccurate energy load forecasts, cyber threats, and energy load-based sustainability issues in smart grids hinder SG-IIoT operations. To mitigate these challenges, a federated learning approach is developed by integrating long short-term memory (LSTM), the transformer-large language model (LLM)-based foundation models, and advanced encryption standard (AES) cryptography. The proposed approach is named the responsible federated foundation model (ResFedFM). To ensure secure federated learning computation and data security at the edge (smart meter), fog (SCADA-based substation grid), and cloud (grid cloud server) layers of the SG-IIoT, a self-parent key-based cryptography method has been developed by combining AES with hash-based message authentication code (HMAC). A load forecasting algorithm called LSTM-LLM-GenResAI-Forecasting has been designed for computation at each end node of the federated learning process. The edge node forecast outputs are encrypted and aggregated at the fog node. At the fog node, the data are decrypted, and the aggregation algorithm of the federated learning process is used to generate the overall load forecasting of each sub-station grid. Again, the forecast data from these fog nodes are aggregated in an encrypted state at the cloud level, and overall load forecasts are generated for multiple fog nodes. The result of the proposed approach provides responsible forecasting (high accuracy, green computing-based energy demand, optimization of AI-hallucination, and grid data security), demonstrating enhanced performance over seven significant models.
